{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P3Lr6epbvZNMCfCpVASUe9","type":"tool_result","content":"3000:[open_item.OI-70]\n3040:- BUT the lane closed itself COMPLETE on its own branch (spec at docs/archive/work/complete/, active/ empty there), and the integrator reads the outcome off the spec's directory — as it stands the branch asserts `merged` and the gate applies. The (c) execution therefore first CONVERTS the close: revert the lane's own close commit (or re-perform the close into partial/) so the outcome reads handback and the report mints the disposition row through the ordinary path. No bespoke rename, per OI-70's direction.\n3055:recommendation = \"\"\"Superseded by the owner direction: (c). Next steps in order: (1) file the two renderer-defect rows (trunk-side, independent of the lane); (2) convert the lane's complete-close to a partial close as above and let the handback report, merge and disposition row run through the kit's own path; (3) the disposition's adjudicator judges keep/discard under OI-70's bounds and queues the successor — which inherits OI-72's ruling for the SR-163 shape and re-lands the reviewed spine content (about 4 lines of low-level-requirements.toml and 10 of test-cases.toml, both auto-merging clean per the handoff §1.4). CLARITY ANSWERED (owner, 2026-08-31): this close is a special case for how the lane got lost — perform the partial close MANUALLY (the complete-close conversion above), then open a successor WI pointing at the preserved record, provided the churn does not risk degrading the output. That risk was assessed this session: the successor must NOT re-land the docs/archive/last_approved bytes from the branch; it re-lands the LIVE registry edits and regenerates the snapshot with the kit's only sanctioned writer (intake.py snapshot) at its own approval commit. Decision 9 measured that writer reproducing the lane's snapshot byte-identical from live state, so regenerate-not-copy carries no degradation risk and the handoff §1.6 snapshot question stays a historical note on the held branch rather than a live hazard on trunk.\"\"\"\n3083:title = \"Typed OI dependencies and the mandatory successor: the ordering OI-70's two exits left open\"\n3089:decision = \"\"\"RULED 2026-08-31 (record docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-ruling-oi73.md): (a), the full posture as recommended — the owner: \"Agreed with recommendation, please update the frontier and current WI scope as needed.\" OI ids become valid hard tokens in needs, satisfied when the row leaves pending; every PARTIAL or CANCELLED disposition must queue at least one successor (an OI alone no longer discharges the refusal invariant — OI-70's exit-(B)-alone case is retired); the mint REPLACES the superseded row's inbound hard needs edges; dead_dependency_findings extends to partial as the validator net. WI-552 is re-scoped in the ruling commit to own all four arms; no new row.\n3091:The original brief follows. Two gaps measured against the 2026-08-31 hand-back mechanization, raised the same day the OI-70/71/72 rulings landed:\n3093:(1) A MIXED OUTCOME HAS NO ORDERING. OI-70 permits one close to queue a successor AND mint an OI, and WI-552 builds both arms — but nothing ties the successor's READINESS to the OI's RULING. The only mechanized OI->WI link is advisory: intake.context_block injects pending OIs whose wi_refs intersect the session's scope as premise-risk context, and is explicitly never gating. A successor whose premise awaits a human answer is schedulable the moment it lands in queued/; today only adjudicator judgment holds it back.\n3097:THE OWNER'S POSTURE (2026-08-31, stated when raising this item): simplify. Every partial close MUST queue at least one successor WI; inbound hard needs edges on the closed row are REPLACED with the successor at the mint; and the successor MAY carry an OI id in needs where the answer is human-owed and the adjudicator found no alternative route — the OI exit becomes a typed dependency of the successor rather than a standalone exit. An OI edge is hard and is satisfied when the row leaves pending. This refines OI-70 rather than re-litigating it: BlockRef was retired as free text with zero producers; a validated OI edge read by the loaders, the validator and the scheduler is a different instrument.\"\"\"\n3098:blast_radius = \"\"\"Refines a ruling made the same day: OI-70's exit-(B)-alone case disappears in favor of successor-plus-typed-edge, and WI-552's Done-when 2-4 must be amended BEFORE that row is worked — it sits on the ready frontier now (the wi_refs here inject this item as premise risk into any session that claims it, the advisory net until the ruling). Mechanically: the needs token grammar (registries/work-items.template.csv and the PROCESS_OPTIONS dependency prose), both loaders (check_trajectory.load_wis, schedule.load_wis), the validator's predecessor-resolution ERROR arm, scheduler readiness (hard_preds_satisfied, _disposition, a new waiting reason), and the OI carrier becoming a readiness input for the first time. The shipped template's grammar widens tolerantly — bare WI ids keep meaning what they mean, so no downstream flag day.\"\"\"\n3100:- (b) TYPED EDGES ONLY: allow OI ids in needs but keep OI-70's exits exactly as ruled — the OI-only exit stays, and Done-when 4 stays re-point-or-loudly-report. FOR: the smaller amendment; gap (1) closes. AGAINST: gap (2)'s OI-only window stays open, carried only by the validator warning.\n3101:- (c) MANDATORY SUCCESSOR ONLY: no typed edges; every partial close queues a successor and edges are replaced; the human-owed case is successor plus OI with the ordering held by convention. FOR: the WI graph stays single-registry. AGAINST: gap (1) survives — which is today's silent state, the ritual a tired session skips (OI-70 option (c)'s own AGAINST).\n3102:- (d) NEITHER: keep OI-70 as ruled and WI-552 as filed; adjudicator judgment carries the ordering, the hand repair carries the strands. FOR: zero code, zero amendment. AGAINST: both gaps are already measured failures, one repaired by hand this week.\"\"\"\n3112:one_line = \"\"\"RULED ((a) as amended by the owner: a PURE-TOML per-run record, review state changed IN PLACE, no second record to join) 2026-08-31 — one TOML file per delegated run, entries as tables with required keys plus a review cell defaulting empty that the owner edits with any free-string note; a non-empty review marks the entry reviewed, and no machinery checks the review state. Original brief: rule the decide-and-record-for-affirmation mechanism — per-sitting run report vs per-decision registry, where affirmation lands, what an overturn mints, which altitudes it covers; a RECORD beside OI-70/OI-73's exits, never a third exit\"\"\"\n3113:decision = \"\"\"RULED 2026-08-31 (record docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi74-75.md): (a), the per-sitting run report, with the owner amending the format and the review lifecycle — PURE TOML, not the markdown-plus-frontmatter hybrid, and review state changed in place rather than recorded beside it. The owner: \"if it was toml, the state could just change (and no machinery needs to check it, it can be up to the user to make whatever string note they want so some eventual (if ever) mechanized collator of all decisions could skip any that already has some review entry. I would pref that over yet another record that needs to be joined.\" The driver's own registry is the evidence the hybrid was unnecessary: this file authors long prose in TOML strings already, and one-file-per-run keeps the medium conflict-free the way log.d fragments are. What survives from the recommendation: the run is the unit; every delegated run closes with one record; entries carry REQUIRED keys (decided, alternative, reversal_cost, why_not_escalated) so the format cannot degrade the way the prose contract measurably did; a high-risk hoist gives per-decision visibility; an overturned entry mints a WI; all delegated altitudes are covered; and the record is never an exit — OI-70/OI-73's machinery still owns where work goes. What changes: each entry also carries review = \"\" (empty = unreviewed; any owner string = reviewed, semantics the owner's own); the file is edited in place for review and is NOT immutable; no separate approval record, no join, and NO review-state checker or surface band is commissioned — an eventual collator, if ever built, skips entries whose review is non-empty. Commissioned: WI-557 (with OI-75's dial).\n3115:The original brief follows. The evidence base is docs/knowledge/decision-routing.md (2026-08-31 research, external plus this repo's own measurements). The problem: sessions and adjudicators make delegated decisions that deserve a non-blocking owner second look, and today that practice has no home — too settled for a pending OI (blocking by design, and scheduling-gating since OI-73), not settled enough for the log's Decisions section (already-settled decisions only). The ad-hoc prototype ran three times (docs/decisions-for-review-*.md, 72 entries) with a measured 4/72 affirmation rate, no script or check reading any file, and the declared prose format degrading within one session (21 of 46 entries carried the Decided field). The one affirmation that ever happened was PER-SITTING with per-entry exceptions, recorded as an approval commit quoting the owner's words. 44 of the 46 most recent entries were SUPERVISOR-altitude decisions about running the loop — an altitude OI-70's adjudicator-exits doctrine does not reach. Framing constraint: OI-70 ruled exactly two exits, no third, nothing silent; this mechanism must be a record of calls already made and discharged (or needing no exit at all), or it re-litigates that ruling. The review-queue literature in the pack says a mostly-fine per-entry queue decays into reflex regardless of reviewer diligence; what survives is a filtered, counted, batched surface landing where the owner already stops.\"\"\"\n3116:blast_radius = \"\"\"Whichever shape lands touches the owner surface (a new generated band with derived counts), a close-time existence check for delegated runs, and the supervisor/session doctrine (session-protocol skill, the embedded resume prompt). Shape (b) additionally costs a new registry, id space, watermark row, template, schema check, and a resync entry — the heaviest artifact class the kit has, two weeks after OI-73's one-word posture (simplify). Shape (a) reuses the dated human-approval record convention and the handback-style immutable close artifact. Getting the framing wrong re-opens OI-70 within the month; getting the surface wrong reproduces the measured 4/72 graveyard with a schema.\"\"\"\n3121:recommendation = \"\"\"(a), covering ALL delegated altitudes (supervisor sessions, adjudicators, workers — any session that ends a delegated run writes the report), with the entry contract as REQUIRED STRUCTURED FIELDS (what was decided, the alternative passed over, reversal cost, why it was not escalated) so the format cannot degrade the way the prose contract did, and the high-risk hoist giving the owner per-decision visibility without a per-decision queue. Overturn mints a WI (an overturned decision is work to undo, not a question); affirmation is the existing dated-approval ritual, per sitting, exceptions listed. State in the built doctrine, explicitly, that this is a record and not an exit — OI-70/OI-73 remain the only exits. The owner's separately noted in-HTML feedback idea (commit 1983e05a) has its natural host in the generated band, and folding it in is the owner's call at the ruling.\"\"\"\n3131:decision = \"\"\"RULED 2026-08-31 (record docs/log.d/2026-08-31-owner-rulings-oi74-75.md): (b) — the owner chose the dial now over the recommendation's defer-and-measure: \"Hmmm, this is the interesting dial. I would say 2.\" One key in [attestation]: decision_recording = \"off\" | \"record\" | \"escalate-first\" — off: no recording obligation (the template's shipped value, conservative default); record: a delegated run's close OWES the OI-74 decisions record, the way a partial close owes its handback report (this repo's value); escalate-first: beyond recording, sessions prefer the OI-70/OI-73 exits over deciding — delegate less, ask more. Single line, closed alphabet, IF-037 grep-parity respected, structural parity template-vs-repo under the dogfood-sync test. The doctrine rides with it as ruled in the brief: routing on action fields and counts (never model self-confidence as a primary; confidence and panel dissent promote scrutiny only, never demote), the disclosure obligation as OI-74's required entry keys, and the dial as a workload-allocation instrument that never moves the safety boundary — the escalate class binds at every setting. Commissioned: WI-557 (with OI-74's record).\n3139:recommendation = \"\"\"(c), with (d) folded into the later step if volume warrants: rule the doctrine now — action-fields routing, capacity-bounded record tier, confidence as one-way ratchet only, the disclosure obligation as a required structured field with a refusal for silence — and revisit the dial in one sitting after the OI-74 surface has carried a few real runs. Whatever is chosen, the dial or prompt is a workload-allocation instrument and never the safety boundary: the escalate class (OI-70/OI-73's exits) binds at every setting.\"\"\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1224d915-a66e-46ad-b7b8-5a1783716521","uuid":"d60c0675-3ea8-456f-a75c-267331381509","timestamp":"2026-09-01T10:00:55.505Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"3000:[open_item.OI-70]\n3040:- BUT the lane closed itself COMPLETE on its own branch (spec at docs/archive/work/complete/, active/ empty there), and the integrator reads the outcome off the spec's directory — as it stands the branch asserts `merged` and the gate applies. The (c) execution therefore first CONVERTS the close: revert the lane's own close commit (or re-perform the close into partial/) so the outcome reads handback and the report mints the disposition row through the ordinary path. No bespoke rename, per OI-70's direction.\n3055:recommendation = \"\"\"Superseded by the owner direction: (c). Next steps in order: (1) file the two renderer-defect rows (trunk-side, independent of the lane); (2) convert the lane's complete-close to a partial close as above and let the handback report, merge and disposition row run through the kit's own path; (3) the disposition's adjudicator judges keep/discard under OI-70's bounds and queues the successor — which inherits OI-72's ruling for the SR-163 shape and re-lands the reviewed spine content (about 4 lines of low-level-requirements.toml and 10 of test-cases.toml, both auto-merging clean per the handoff §1.4). CLARITY ANSWERED (owner, 2026-08-31): this close is a special case for how the lane got lost — perform the partial close MANUALLY (the complete-close conversion above), then open a successor WI pointing at the preserved record, provided the churn does not risk degrading the output. That risk was assessed this session: the successor m
